---
<p>Mina Shah orders a blue mug and two packets of green tea from a small online shop. To her it is one action. To the shop it is several facts that must change together: a new order exists, it belongs to Mina, it contains two lines at agreed prices, the last mug is no longer in stock and nothing has been paid yet. At the same moment Tariq Aziz is looking at the same mug, a report is totalling yesterday’s sales and a nightly job is tidying old baskets. Every one of them must see facts that agree with each other.</p>
<p>A relational database exists to keep facts like these correct while many people read and change them. It stores facts in tables, refuses changes that break rules you have declared, answers questions you describe rather than program, applies a group of changes completely or not at all, and keeps committed changes through a crash. It does not decide what the facts mean, which rules matter, what the application should do when something fails, or how to recover from a mistake that was itself committed. Those decisions stay with you, and most expensive database incidents begin where someone assumed they did not.</p>
<p>This article gives you a working model of the whole subject using that one shop. Each section answers its question well enough to act on, then names the article that takes it further. Start with this introduction. Then read each longer article before the shorter articles that follow from it, beginning with these four in order: <a href={`${base}/data-design/`}>Designing relational data</a>, <a href={`${base}/sql/`}>Working with SQL</a>, <a href={`${base}/transactions/`}>Making concurrent changes correct</a> and <a href={`${base}/applications/`}>Building applications around a relational database</a>. The shorter articles each answer one narrower question, so you can leave one until its question matters to you. If you arrived for a particular task, the last section gives a short route, and each shorter article names, at its start, the section of the longer article it assumes.</p>

<nav class="article-contents" aria-label="In this article">
  <ol>{sections.map(([id, title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol>
</nav>

<h2 id="one-action-changes-several-facts">One order changes several facts</h2>
<p>Suppose the shop kept its data in files, and the application wrote Mina’s order as a sequence of separate writes: add the order, add the order lines, reduce the stock. Most of the time all three writes finish. The question that matters is what happens when they do not, and what happens when two orders are written at once. The table below follows each case. Its outcomes are reasoned out, not observed.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where a sequence of separate writes can stop"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>What happens</th><th>What is left behind</th><th>What is now wrong</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The work stops after adding the order</td><td>Order 103 with no order lines; stock unchanged</td><td>An empty order that may be charged, shipped or counted</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The work stops after adding the order lines</td><td>A complete order; the mug still shows 1 in stock</td><td>Tariq can buy a mug that has already been sold</td></tr>
    <tr><td>All three writes finish</td><td>Everything Mina asked for</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mina’s and Tariq’s orders are written at the same time</td><td>Both read 1 mug in stock, and both orders finish</td><td>Two orders hold the one mug</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The last row is the harder problem. Every write finished, yet two orders interleaved over the same stock each acted on a number the other was about to change. Files give the application no help with either problem. A relational database takes on three jobs that address them:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>It enforces declared rules.</strong> If you state that stock cannot be negative or that every order line refers to a real product, the database refuses any change that would break the rule, whichever program sends it.</li>
  <li><strong>It runs changes as transactions.</strong> A group of statements either commits as a whole or leaves no trace, and concurrent transactions are kept apart according to a stated isolation level.</li>
  <li><strong>It keeps what it has committed.</strong> Once a commit is acknowledged, the change survives a crash of the database process or the machine, within limits the configuration states.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these jobs only covers what you express. A rule you never declared, such as “a paid order’s lines must not change”, is enforced by nobody. A transaction protects the statements inside it, not the payment request your application sends somewhere else. The working model is therefore a division of labour:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What the database does and what you still decide"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>The database does</th><th>You still decide</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Stores rows in tables with declared types</td><td>What each row means and which table each fact belongs in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Refuses changes that break declared keys and constraints</td><td>Which rules to declare, and how the application reports a refusal</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Finds and combines rows for a query</td><td>Whether the query asks the question you meant</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Applies a transaction completely or not at all, at a stated isolation level</td><td>What belongs in one transaction, and what to do when it fails or its outcome is unknown</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Keeps committed changes through a crash, as configured</td><td>Which failures and mistakes you must recover from, and proving that you can</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The rest of this article walks along that table, from the shape of the data to the day something goes wrong.</p>

<h2 id="tables-describe-facts-and-relationships">Tables, keys and relationships</h2>
<p>A table holds one kind of fact. Each row is one instance of that fact, and each column is one property of it. The shop needs five tables: customers, products, orders, order lines (the <code>order_items</code> table) and payments. Mina’s name is stored once, in <code>customers</code>. Her orders do not repeat it; they store her <code>customer_id</code>, and anything that needs her name follows that number to the customer row.</p>
<p>The number that identifies a row is its <strong>primary key</strong>. No two rows in a table can share one, so “customer 1” always means exactly one customer. A column that stores another table’s key is a <strong>foreign key</strong>: the database checks that the row it refers to exists, and refuses a change that would leave a reference pointing at nothing. An order line’s key is a pair, the order number and the line number, because “line 1” only means something inside one order. Keys are what turn five separate tables into one consistent description of the shop.</p>
<KeysFigure />
<p>Storing each fact once is the point, not a tidiness preference. If Mina’s name were copied into every order, changing it would mean finding every copy, and a missed copy would leave the shop disagreeing with itself. When you need the facts together, a query joins the rows back up by matching keys. This one rebuilds Mina’s receipt:</p>
<Sql code={receiptSql} label="SQL: rebuild the receipt for order 103" />
<div class="table-scroll a00-compact" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Receipt for order 103, as PostgreSQL 18.6 returned it"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>order_<wbr />id</th><th>customer</th><th>line_<wbr />no</th><th>product</th><th>quantity</th><th>unit_<wbr />price</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{receiptRows.map(r => <tr>{r.map(v => <td>{v}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Notice that each line stores its own <code>unit_price</code>. That looks like a copy of the product’s price, but it is a different fact: the price Mina agreed to, which must not change when the shop raises its prices next month. Deciding whether two values are one fact or two is most of data design, and no database can do it for you.</p>
<p>The schema also declares rules. These statements were sent to the shop in PostgreSQL 18.6 after order 103 had committed. Each refusal comes back with a SQLSTATE code, a five-character error class that applications can test instead of parsing messages.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Changes the declared rules refused, observed in PostgreSQL 18.6"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Attempted change</th><th>Rule that applies</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{rejections.map(r => <tr><td>{r.what}<br /><code>{r.sql}</code></td><td>{r.rule}</td><td>{r.sqlstate ? `Refused: ${r.refusal} (${r.sqlstate})` : 'Accepted'}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The last row matters as much as the others. Changing an order line of an order that has already been paid is almost certainly wrong for this shop, but no declared rule says so, and the database accepted it. Some rules can be declared as constraints; some need a trigger, a narrower permission or application code; some are better made impossible by the shape of the tables. Knowing which rules you have actually expressed, and where, is part of owning the data.</p>
<p>Two more ideas complete the first model. A column can hold <strong>NULL</strong>, which means “no value” rather than zero or an empty string, and any arithmetic or comparison involving NULL produces NULL rather than true or false. Declare <code>NOT NULL</code> wherever a value must exist. And the tables you declare are a <strong>logical</strong> description: they say what facts exist, not how rows are laid out on disk. The database chooses the physical layout, which is why the same query can become slow as the table grows without anyone changing it, as <a href="#correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">The work behind a query</a> shows.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/data-design/`}>Designing relational data</a> takes this further: <a href={`${base}/data-design/#give-every-row-a-clear-meaning`}>giving every row a clear meaning</a>, <a href={`${base}/data-design/#make-invalid-facts-difficult-to-store`}>making invalid facts difficult to store</a> and <a href={`${base}/data-design/#store-each-fact-where-it-belongs`}>storing each fact where it belongs</a>. Its shorter companion articles cover <a href={`${base}/types/`}>Choosing types and representing missing values</a>, <a href={`${base}/constraints/`}>Expressing rules with constraints</a>, <a href={`${base}/normalisation/`}>Normalisation and deliberate redundancy</a> and <a href={`${base}/history/`}>Modelling history and data lifecycles</a>.</p>

<h2 id="ask-for-a-result-then-check-what-it-means">Asking questions with SQL</h2>
<p>SQL is the language you use to describe the rows you want and the changes you intend. It is <strong>declarative</strong>: you state the result, not the steps to compute it. This query asks for Mina’s orders, oldest first:</p>
<Sql code={selectSql} label="SQL: Mina’s orders, oldest first" />
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Mina’s orders, as PostgreSQL 18.6 returned them"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>order_id</th><th>placed_at</th><th>status</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{selectRows.map(r => <tr>{r.map(v => <td>{v}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The query says nothing about which file to open, whether to use an index or in what order to visit rows; the database works that out. The query also says <code>ORDER BY</code>, because a table has no inherent order. Without it, rows come back in whatever order the chosen plan produces, and that order can change from one day to the next.</p>
<p>Being declarative has a cost that newcomers rarely expect: a query that runs without error has only proved that it is valid SQL. It has not proved that it answers your question. Figure 2 asks one plain question of the shop, three ways.</p>
<QueryFigure />
<p>The first query is the dangerous one. Its numbers look reasonable, it returns quickly and nothing warns you that order 101 has been counted twice and order 103 has gone. The defence is a habit, not a feature: before trusting a query, check it against a small case you can calculate by hand, including the awkward rows, such as an order with several payments and an order with none. The cause is also worth naming, because it recurs everywhere. Joining two independent lists that both belong to one order, here its order lines and its payments, multiplies them; summarise each list first, then join the summaries.</p>
<p>The same discipline applies to changes, where the stakes are higher. Suppose Mina asks to cancel her pending order. Both of these statements are valid:</p>
<Sql code={broadUpdateSql} label="SQL: a cancellation that is too broad" />
<Sql code={narrowUpdateSql} label="SQL: a cancellation limited to the intended row" />
<p>In PostgreSQL 18.6 the first reported <code>{broadUpdateResult}</code>: it cancelled order 103 and also her paid order 101. The second reported <code>{narrowUpdateResult}</code>. The count of affected rows is the cheapest check SQL gives you. Know how many rows a change should touch before you run it, run it inside a transaction you can roll back, and read the count before you commit.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/sql/`}>Working with SQL</a> builds this into a method, starting from <a href={`${base}/sql/#start-with-a-database-you-can-inspect-and-reset`}>a database you can inspect and reset</a>, through <a href={`${base}/sql/#combine-related-facts-without-changing-the-question`}>combining related facts without changing the question</a> and <a href={`${base}/sql/#check-the-answer-before-trusting-the-query`}>checking the answer</a>, to <a href={`${base}/sql/#change-the-intended-rows-and-inspect-the-outcome`}>changing only the intended rows</a>. Its shorter companion articles cover <a href={`${base}/sql-values/`}>Getting SQL values right</a>, <a href={`${base}/joins-and-aggregation/`}>Joining and summarising data</a>, <a href={`${base}/query-composition/`}>Composing larger SQL queries</a>, <a href={`${base}/window-functions/`}>Calculating across related rows</a> and <a href={`${base}/changing-data/`}>Changing data with SQL</a>.</p>

<h2 id="a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">Transactions and competing buyers</h2>
<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups statements so that they take effect together. Mina’s order becomes one:</p>
<Sql code={orderTransactionSql} label="SQL: Mina’s order as one transaction" />
<p>Between <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code>, nothing Mina’s transaction does is visible to anyone else. If the application crashes, the connection drops or the code issues <code>ROLLBACK</code>, every statement is undone and the shop looks as if the order had never been attempted. That solves the first two rows of the table in <a href="#one-action-changes-several-facts">One order changes several facts</a>, where the work stopped part-way. It does not, by itself, solve the last row: two complete, correct-looking transactions running at once.</p>
<p>What one transaction sees of another while both are running is set by its <strong>isolation level</strong>. At <strong>Read Committed</strong>, PostgreSQL’s default, each statement sees the data as committed when that statement began. At <strong>Repeatable Read</strong>, every statement in the transaction sees one <strong>snapshot</strong>: the data as committed when the transaction’s first statement ran. Separately, a transaction that changes a row holds a <strong>lock</strong> on that row until it commits or rolls back, so a second transaction that tries to change the same row waits.</p>
<p>Notice the condition <code>stock &gt;= 1</code> in the update. Figure 3 shows why it is there. Mina and Tariq both try to buy the last blue mug, with their transactions interleaved in exactly the same order each time. Only the way each transaction takes the mug, and the isolation level, changes between the options.</p>
<RaceFigure />
<p>Read the first option closely, because it is the most common concurrency bug in applications that use databases. Each transaction read the stock, decided in application code that a mug was available and wrote back a number it had calculated. Both transactions were atomic. Both committed. The shop sold one mug twice and its stock count says nothing is wrong. Transactions did not fail; they were never asked to protect the decision.</p>
<p>The other two options protect it in different ways. In the guarded version, the condition and the change travel together in one statement, so the database rechecks the condition against the committed row once Mina’s lock is released, and the application learns from <code>UPDATE 0</code> that it lost. In the Repeatable Read version, the database refuses to let Tariq overwrite a row that changed after his snapshot was taken. His update fails with a <strong>serialisation failure</strong> (SQLSTATE 40001), and the application must roll back and retry the whole transaction. Both are correct. They put different work on the application: the guarded statement needs a check of the row count, and the stricter isolation level needs a retry loop.</p>
<p>In this shop the <code>CHECK (stock &gt;= 0)</code> constraint is a last line of defence. With a plain <code>stock = stock - 1</code> in place of the guarded statement, PostgreSQL 18.6 refused Tariq’s change with a check violation (23514) instead of letting stock go negative. It could not have caught the first option, which never made stock negative.</p>
<p>The isolation level is a contract, and its names do not mean the same thing everywhere. PostgreSQL starts transactions at Read Committed unless told otherwise. MySQL’s InnoDB defaults to Repeatable Read, but its documentation states that the snapshot applies only to plain reads: updates and locking reads act on the latest committed rows. So the second option’s refusal is PostgreSQL behaviour, not something to expect from InnoDB under the same name. Check the documented behaviour of the engine you use, at the level you use, for the pattern you rely on.</p>
<p>A commit also has boundaries. Before acknowledging a commit, PostgreSQL appends a record of every change to its <strong>write-ahead log</strong>, a file it can replay after a crash, and makes sure that record has reached durable storage. It copies the changed pages into the table files later. So with the default settings, a crash immediately after the acknowledgement loses nothing. With <code>synchronous_commit = off</code>, PostgreSQL replies before the log record reaches storage, so a crash can lose the last fraction of a second of commits.</p>
<p>Beyond the database, nothing is protected. If the order transaction also charges Mina’s card, a rollback cannot refund the charge. The usual pattern has three steps. Commit the order as <code>pending</code>. Ask the payment provider to charge it with an idempotency key, so a repeated request cannot charge twice. Record the outcome in a later transaction. More generally, work that must follow a commit is written, in the same transaction, to an outbox table, and a separate process carries it out afterwards.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/transactions/`}>Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops each part: how <a href={`${base}/transactions/#two-correct-looking-transactions-can-make-an-incorrect-result`}>two correct-looking transactions make an incorrect result</a>, how to <a href={`${base}/transactions/#express-a-change-and-its-condition-together`}>express a change and its condition together</a>, <a href={`${base}/transactions/#understand-the-isolation-contract-behind-the-choice`}>the isolation contract</a>, and <a href={`${base}/transactions/#commit-has-a-durability-boundary-and-a-system-boundary`}>the durability and system boundaries of a commit</a>. Its shorter companion articles are <a href={`${base}/locking/`}>Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> and <a href={`${base}/isolation/`}>Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a>, and <a href={`${base}/background-work/`}>Connecting transactions to background work and events</a> covers the outbox.</p>

<h2 id="the-application-is-part-of-the-database-system">From a request to a committed order</h2>
<p>Mina does not type SQL. She presses a button, a web request reaches the application, and the application talks to the database over a network connection. Correctness depends on that whole path, and four parts of it decide most outcomes.</p>
<p><strong>Values travel separately from SQL.</strong> The application sends a statement with placeholders and passes Mina’s values as parameters, so the database treats them as data, never as part of the SQL. Building SQL by pasting user input into a string is how SQL injection happens. The application should also connect with an account that can do what the shop needs and nothing more; an account that can drop tables turns any bug into a possible disaster.</p>
<p><strong>A connection is a limited, shared resource.</strong> Each PostgreSQL connection is served by its own server process, so applications borrow connections from a pool and return them quickly. A transaction belongs to one connection. If the code begins a transaction, then waits on a slow payment provider before committing, it keeps that connection, and any row locks the transaction holds, for the whole wait. Under load, those waits become a queue for connections and other buyers blocked behind Mina’s lock. Keep transactions short, and never hold one open across a call to another service or a person. And never return a connection to the pool with a transaction still open: the next request to borrow it would carry on inside that transaction, and its <code>COMMIT</code> would commit the abandoned work too.</p>
<p><strong>Every failure has a known or an unknown outcome.</strong> The application must handle each case differently:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What the application knows after each kind of failure"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>What happened</th><th>What the application knows</th><th>A safe response</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>An error before <code>COMMIT</code>, such as a refused constraint or a serialisation failure</td><td>Nothing was committed</td><td>Report the refusal, or retry the whole transaction if the error is one that retrying can cure</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>COMMIT</code> acknowledged</td><td>The order is committed</td><td>Continue, and tell Mina</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The connection is lost after <code>COMMIT</code> was sent, before the reply</td><td>Nothing: it may or may not have committed</td><td>Look the order up by a request identifier before doing anything else</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The request’s deadline expires before <code>COMMIT</code> was sent</td><td>Nothing is committed, once the transaction is rolled back or the connection closed</td><td>Roll back or discard the connection; never return it to the pool mid-transaction</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The request’s deadline expires while <code>COMMIT</code> is in flight</td><td>Nothing: it may or may not have committed</td><td>Look the order up by its request identifier, as for a lost connection</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The lost-connection and in-flight rows are why careful applications give each request a unique identifier and store it with the order, under a unique constraint. A retried request then finds the order the first attempt created, instead of creating a second one. A lost reply never proves that nothing happened.</p>
<p><strong>The code that generates SQL is still sending SQL.</strong> An object-relational mapper saves typing but can turn one page view into hundreds of small queries, or a single <code>save()</code> into an update of every column. Read the SQL it sends for the requests that matter.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/applications/`}>Building applications around a relational database</a> covers this path: <a href={`${base}/applications/#preserve-values-and-authority-when-sending-sql`}>preserving values and authority</a>, <a href={`${base}/applications/#borrow-a-connection-for-bounded-work`}>borrowing a connection for bounded work</a>, <a href={`${base}/applications/#recover-according-to-what-the-application-knows`}>recovering according to what the application knows</a> and <a href={`${base}/applications/#make-work-beyond-the-transaction-recoverable`}>making work beyond the transaction recoverable</a>. Its shorter companion articles cover <a href={`${base}/connections/`}>Managing connections and transaction lifetimes</a>, <a href={`${base}/generated-queries/`}>Controlling the queries your application performs</a>, <a href={`${base}/request-recovery/`}>Recovering database requests safely</a> and <a href={`${base}/background-work/`}>Connecting transactions to background work and events</a>.</p>

<h2 id="correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">The work behind a query</h2>
<p>A declarative query hands the “how” to the database, but the work still has to happen. Rows live in fixed-size pages, 8 kB each in PostgreSQL, and finding rows means reading pages. To see how that scales, here is an <code>orders</code> table at three sizes, with orders spread evenly across 1,000 customers, and the number of pages PostgreSQL read for each query.</p>
<div class="table-scroll a00-pages" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Pages read to find rows, observed in PostgreSQL 18.6"><table>
  <caption>Observed with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> on PostgreSQL 18.6, parallel workers off. Counts are pages the scan read from cache or storage, not elapsed time.</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Orders in the table</th>{scanObserved.map(s => <th scope="col">{fmt(s.rows)}</th>)}</tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    {([
      ['Table size, pages', 'tablePages'],
      ['One order by its key, pages', 'idPages'],
      ['Rows for one customer', 'customerRows'],
      ['One customer, no index, pages', 'scanPages'],
      ['One customer, with an index, pages', 'indexPages'],
    ] as const).map(([label, key]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th>{scanObserved.map(s => <td>{fmt(s[key])}</td>)}</tr>)}
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Finding one order by its primary key costs three or four pages at every size, because the key has an <strong>index</strong>: a separate, sorted structure (a B-tree in PostgreSQL) that leads to a row in a few steps however large the table grows. Finding one customer’s orders without an index means reading the whole table, and that work grows in step with the table. The query never changed. It was correct at a thousand rows and correct at a million; only its cost changed.</p>
<p>The last column carries a second lesson. With an index on <code>customer_id</code>, PostgreSQL went straight to the customer’s rows, yet at a million orders it still read about a thousand pages, because this customer’s thousand orders are scattered one per page across the table. An index finds rows quickly; it cannot make scattered rows adjacent. Every index also has to be updated by every insert, and by every update that changes an indexed column, so indexes are a trade between reading and writing, not a free improvement.</p>
<p>The database chooses how to run each query by estimating the cost of the alternatives from statistics about the data. <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the plan it chose; <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> runs the query and shows what actually happened, including the estimated and actual row counts at each step. A large gap between those counts is the usual reason a good query gets a bad plan. And a query that is fast alone can still be slow in production, because it waits for locks, for a connection or for disks shared with every other query. Look at the slow tail of response times, not just the average, and measure the workload rather than the query in isolation.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/performance/`}>Understanding and improving database performance</a> turns this into a diagnostic method: <a href={`${base}/performance/#find-where-time-and-work-accumulate`}>find where time and work accumulate</a>, <a href={`${base}/performance/#give-the-query-a-useful-route-to-its-rows`}>give the query a useful route to its rows</a>, <a href={`${base}/performance/#explain-the-work-and-test-a-plan-hypothesis`}>explain the work and test a plan hypothesis</a>, and remember that <a href={`${base}/performance/#a-faster-query-can-still-make-the-workload-slower`}>a faster query can still make the workload slower</a>. Its shorter companion articles cover <a href={`${base}/physical-storage/`}>How stored rows become physical work</a>, <a href={`${base}/indexes/`}>Choosing indexes for a workload</a>, <a href={`${base}/query-plans/`}>Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a> and <a href={`${base}/concurrent-load/`}>Improving performance under concurrent load</a>.</p>

<h2 id="data-and-applications-change-at-different-speeds">Changing a live database</h2>
<p>The shop starts selling abroad and must record a tax country on every order. The obvious statement is <code>ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN tax_country text NOT NULL</code>. On a live service it has three problems at once. The existing orders have no tax country. The application servers are replaced one at a time, so for a while old code that knows nothing about the column runs beside new code that does. And the change itself has to be applied to a table that people are using.</p>
<p>Here is what PostgreSQL 18.6 did with each alternative on the shop’s <code>orders</code> table:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Ways to add a required tax country, observed in PostgreSQL 18.6"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Observed outcome</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Add the column as <code>NOT NULL</code> with no default</td><td>Refused: not-null violation (23502), because existing rows have no value</td><td>Cannot be done in one step on a table with data</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Add it as <code>NOT NULL DEFAULT 'GB'</code></td><td>Accepted; orders 101, 102 and 103 all read GB; the table was not rewritten</td><td>Fast, but asserts that Tariq’s order 102 is British, which nobody checked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Add it as an optional column</td><td>Accepted without rewriting the table; old code’s inserts and new code’s inserts both accepted</td><td>Safe while both versions of the application run</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Require it while some rows are still empty</td><td>Refused: not-null violation (23502)</td><td>The backfill must finish first</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Require it after the backfill</td><td>Accepted; an insert from old code was then refused: not-null violation (23502)</td><td>Only safe once no old code remains</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The sequence that works follows from those outcomes. Add the column as optional. Deploy application code that writes it on every new order. Backfill the existing orders in small batches, using real data where it exists rather than a convenient default. Only when no old code is running and no empty values remain, require the column. <p>PostgreSQL 18 offers a way to separate the fast part of that last step from the slow part. Once no old code is running, add the requirement as <code>NOT VALID</code>, even before the backfill has finished. From that moment PostgreSQL refuses new orders without a tax country, just as the full requirement would, but it does not check the existing rows. When the backfill is done, <code>VALIDATE CONSTRAINT</code> checks them; in PostgreSQL 18.6 both steps behaved this way.</p>
<p>Two hazards remain. Many schema changes need a lock on the whole table. Even a change that holds the lock briefly must first wait for any long transaction already using the table, and while it waits, every query that arrives after it queues behind it. Set a lock timeout so the change gives up rather than stopping the shop. And reversing a schema change does not reverse data: dropping the new column throws away every tax country entered since. Decide before the first irreversible step how you would recover, and keep the information you would need.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/live-changes/`}>Changing a live database</a> covers how to <a href={`${base}/live-changes/#keep-old-and-new-applications-compatible`}>keep old and new applications compatible</a>, <a href={`${base}/live-changes/#find-out-what-the-database-must-do`}>find out what the database must do</a> for a change, and <a href={`${base}/live-changes/#plan-recovery-before-the-first-irreversible-step`}>plan recovery before the first irreversible step</a>. Its shorter companion articles cover <a href={`${base}/schema-changes/`}>Changing schemas without breaking applications</a>, <a href={`${base}/data-changes/`}>Running reliable data changes</a> and <a href={`${base}/database-moves/`}>Moving databases with a controlled cutover</a>.</p>

<h2 id="operate-for-the-failure-you-must-recover-from">Running and recovering the database</h2>
<p>Once the shop is live, someone must keep the database healthy, and that work falls into a few lasting duties. Control who and what can connect, with what privileges, and keep credentials out of code. Leave headroom in connections, memory, storage and input/output, and watch the signals that predict trouble before customers notice: slow queries, lock waits, connection use, storage growth and replication lag. Let the database do its routine maintenance; in PostgreSQL, the old row versions left by updates and deletes are reclaimed by vacuuming, and a table that is never vacuumed grows and slows. Apply minor upgrades and plan major ones.</p>
<p>The duty that matters most is recovery, and it starts with a question: which failures must the shop survive, how much recent data can it afford to lose (the recovery point objective) and how long can it be unavailable (the recovery time objective)? Different failures need different tools. A <strong>replica</strong> is a second server that receives the primary’s write-ahead log as it is written and replays it, so it holds a near-current copy of the data. A <strong>backup</strong> is a base copy of the database taken at one moment, plus the write-ahead log archived since, so it can be replayed forward to a chosen moment. Figure 4 compares them on two failures.</p>
<RecoveryFigure />
<p>The replica answers the loss of a machine quickly, but it faithfully copies every committed mistake, so it cannot answer a bad deletion. The backup can be restored to a chosen moment before the mistake, which PostgreSQL calls point-in-time recovery. Restoring to a separate server and copying back only what was lost keeps the valid orders that arrived after the mistake. Most services need both, and a managed database service runs much of this machinery for you; it does not decide your objectives, test your restores or repair a mistake your application committed.</p>
<p>A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a recovery plan. Restore regularly to a separate server, check the restored data against something you know, and time how long it takes. During an incident, preserve the evidence before repairing: copying a damaged state away first means a failed repair can be retried.</p>
<p><a href={`${base}/operations/`}>Running a relational service</a> starts by asking you to <a href={`${base}/operations/#define-what-the-service-must-survive`}>define what the service must survive</a>, then covers <a href={`${base}/operations/#choose-where-reads-and-writes-can-safely-run`}>where reads and writes can safely run</a>, <a href={`${base}/operations/#prove-that-the-data-can-be-restored`}>proving that the data can be restored</a> and <a href={`${base}/operations/#recover-the-incident-without-destroying-the-evidence`}>recovering without destroying the evidence</a>. Its shorter companion articles cover <a href={`${base}/security/`}>Securing a relational service</a>, <a href={`${base}/monitoring-and-capacity/`}>Monitoring and sizing a relational service</a>, <a href={`${base}/maintenance-and-upgrades/`}>Maintaining and upgrading a relational service</a>, <a href={`${base}/replicas-and-partitions/`}>Operating replicas and partitioned data</a> and <a href={`${base}/backup-and-restore/`}>Backing up and restoring relational data</a>.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">Choosing a database and where to read next</h2>
<p>Everything above describes contracts, and that is how to choose a database. Write down what the shop needs: orders and stock must stay consistent under competing buyers; a confirmed order must survive a crash; the order history must be queried in ways nobody has thought of yet; the service must be restorable to a point before a mistake. A relational database fits when the data is a set of related facts, when several operations must change them together, and when the questions will keep changing. It fits less well when the data is mostly a single huge stream of independent events or a cache that can be rebuilt.</p>
<p>Relational systems share the model in this article but differ in the details that cause incidents. Isolation levels with the same name behave differently, as <a href="#a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">Transactions and competing buyers</a> showed. MySQL commits the current transaction implicitly before most schema changes, so a migration script cannot roll back a schema change together with its data changes there, as it can in PostgreSQL. Planners, available index types and replication and recovery tools all differ. The process boundary differs most of all. SQLite is a library inside the application, ideal for a single program’s own data and poor when many machines must write at once. PostgreSQL and MySQL are servers that many application processes share. Distributed SQL systems spread one database across machines and regions, at the price of more coordination on every transaction. Choose the simplest boundary that meets the stated requirements, then test the finalists with the same application, the same data and the same failures, rather than comparing feature lists.</p>
<p>From here, the route depends on why you came:</p>
